1/1/code.py:171:    Key insight: The move is no longer announced by special marker colors; the solver must infer source and target roles by comparing visible stack heights across lanes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D16/0/code.py:62:  - Across 2-4 examples, the human-visible difference is that the marker stands alone instead of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D5/1/code.py:44:#   excessive clutter and to preserve repeated evidence across frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/22/code.py:123:    \"\"\"Smallest key across all rotation/flip variants.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D10/1/code.py:57:#       of the bars\" is stable across all examples and easy to read.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:35:#    never touch each other, and never cross the classifier boundary used by the solver.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:45:#    a separate pool and intentionally repeat across both halves so the human solver can reject color matching.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:252:    # Guarantee at least one repeated color across both halves, so color alone cannot explain the tally rows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D12/2/code.py:61:#   - `rect_col` inference assumes that color is globally most common across all piece\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D10/0/code.py:67:    - Outside that box, disconnected same-color sprites for the key colors are scattered across the grid, with an optional distractor color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/2/code.py:39:#     across lanes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/2/code.py:44:#   - Across examples, the left extreme lane consistently turns grey while the right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:28:#     every sprite switches to the opposite marker prototype at the same scale\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:49:#   - marker motion across the body, not just a color change in place\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:61:#     scales cleanly and is identical across the two prototypes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:73:#   is a red top marker versus a green bottom marker. Across examples, the output consistently flips\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D16/2/code.py:64:#    Across input/output examples, the user can see several fragments with the same kind\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:47:#     - Colors are deliberately reused across multiple shapes, otherwise D16 would\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:146:    \"\"\"Smallest key across all rotation/flip variants.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:543:    - Outside the frame are scattered monochrome sprites; each sprite shape matches one hole family up to rotation or reflection, and colors are reused across multiple families.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:551:    - Shape still determines which sprites are eligible, but color is resolved by a global shrinking inventory. Early large holes can spend a rare color and force later smaller holes to switch to a different eligible color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D4/0/code.py:66:    - Outside that box, disconnected sprites of the key colors are scattered across the background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D9/0/code.py:36:#      single example and obvious across a small ARC training set.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/0/code.py:40:#      distributes keys across separate cells, so the solver must infer row spans and column spans from full-black\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/0/code.py:51:#    across 2-4 examples. No color-id ordering is used; cell order is purely row-major in visible space.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D7/3/code.py:71:    - Horizontal placement is mirrored across the full grid width:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D4/0/code.py:63:    - rect_col is still the most common color across piece pixels because border\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D4/0/code.py:93:      rectangle interior, which is easy to spot across examples\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D4/0/code.py:110:      source for the new color and gives a stable explanation across samples\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D4/3/code.py:64:    - Outside that box, separate monochrome sprites of those key colors are scattered across the background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/14/code.py:37:#   distractors differ by only one cell. Across a few examples this makes the combined\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/1/code.py:49:    - A black rectangular legend box contains one row of key-color pixels across its topmost non-black row.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D3/3/code.py:231:    - rect_col is still the most common color across piece pixels and all four outer sides\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D6/2/code.py:70:#   obvious across 2-3 examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D9/3/code.py:58:#   them, and the marker count progression across frames forms a repeated pattern.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:19:#    now shows a 5-cell blue cross centered at that landing cell. Example: if two shooters\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:24:#    cross is visible. The generator therefore requires at least one collision event and at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:25:#    least one lone bg landing, so each sample contains both a blue cross and a maroon\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:34:#    - Unconditional recoloring of a single cell was harmless before. With a 5-cell cross,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:39:#    Every collision center is chosen away from the border so its 4-neighbor cross is fully\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:40:#    in-bounds. The generator reserves the whole cross footprint for each collision, and no\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:44:#    The blue cross is strictly more salient than the old blue dot, and the presence of at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:47:#    cross. No color-id arithmetic or invisible ordering is used.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:62:#      only full in-bounds crosses, although the solver still clips safely for robustness.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:123:def _collision_cross(center: tuple[int, int]) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], ...]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:128:def _paint_collision_cross(out: np.ndarray, center: tuple[int, int], color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:130:    for r, c in _collision_cross(center):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:146:    - If multiple projectiles land on the same empty background cell, that collision creates a blue (1) explosion cross centered on the landing cell, extending 1 step in the four cardinal directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:148:    Key insight: Triangle orientation still reveals the hidden landing cell, but now repeated landings on the same empty spot are identified by a 5-cell blue cross rather than a single blue pixel.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:188:    # Projectile landings on empty background: lone impacts are maroon, repeated impacts explode into a blue cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:193:            _paint_collision_cross(out, dest, 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:248:def _cross_is_full_in_bounds(center: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:249:    return all(0 <= r < H and 0 <= c < W for r, c in _collision_cross(center))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:296:    footprint = set(_collision_cross(dest))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:297:    return _cross_is_full_in_bounds(dest) and not (footprint & occupied) and not (footprint & reserved_marks)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:391:        reserved_marks.update(_collision_cross(dest))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/0/code.py:433:            _paint_collision_cross(out, dest, COLLISION_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/2/code.py:35:#         legends are intentionally spread across the full input grid rather than merged\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D3/2/code.py:51:#     - rect_col remains shared across both targets to preserve the original wildcard edge logic.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/12/code.py:53:#    - Across examples, humans can see that some bg gaps on the frame edge remain blank while inner cavities get\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D15/0/code.py:65:     the same across all examples, and the legend provides the full mapping table needed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D15/0/code.py:49:#         across all rows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D15/0/code.py:78:#     - using one reserved tally color makes the change immediately visible across\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D3/3/code.py:20:#            evenly across that list instead of packing them at the bottom\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D3/3/code.py:25:#         this is a centered equal-width bin rule; it spreads placements across the whole empty region\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D3/3/code.py:145:    - The destination does not pack the replacements at the bottom; instead, it spreads them evenly across that lane's empty lattice slots.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D15/1/code.py:32:#    - `rect_col = most common across piece pixels`: this remains valid and becomes even\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D15/1/code.py:46:#    - Across 2-4 ARC examples a human can notice that the assembled rectangle is mostly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D15/1/code.py:56:#      seam dots must line up across matching pieces.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/1/code.py:31:#       collisions so the rule is learnable from a few ARC examples instead of spreading evidence across too\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D15/0/code.py:150:    - The operation is driven by marked lane geometry: counts move from red lanes to the green lane, while color duplication across unrelated lanes is a distractor and should not affect which lane donates or receives sprites.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D14/0/code.py:55:#    Across 2-4 examples, humans can compare input and output and notice that the input\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D15/2/code.py:63:#    Across 2-4 examples, rows with counts >= 3 show a repeated key/black/key pattern,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/80a900e0/D5/2/code.py:32:#     - original adjacent-marker extraction is safe only if nearby markers do not merge across colors; here we extract outer components per color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D13/0/code.py:45:#      do not overlap any real sprite, do not cross borders, and keep the padding rule.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D13/0/code.py:47:#    - Across examples, humans can see that only the repeated anchor-colored family swaps.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D6/1/code.py:21:#    repeated across all sprites and across both structural families, so 2-4 examples are enough for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D6/1/code.py:44:#    Only three variable colors participate in the cycle. They appear repeatedly across all sprites and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D6/1/code.py:132:    - The output does not switch a sprite into the other structural family anymore. The only change is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D19/1/code.py:45:# - The sprite template stays shared across frames so the task still looks like\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D19/1/code.py:298:      background is the most common color across the full grid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D10/2/code.py:28:#    - Each sprite gets an ordered pair of colors with no reuse across sprites, so humans can track\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D10/2/code.py:112:    - Geometry is shared across all sprites, so the only thing that circulates is the full color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D10/2/code.py:280:    \"\"\"Pick count ordered color pairs with no reuse across sprites.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D17/1/code.py:21:#    The role system stays small and repeated across every sample: red = move, green =\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D17/1/code.py:37:#    The generator enforces total input sprites across all four lanes <= 6, so the green\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D17/1/code.py:44:#    of donor, but the copy batch is separated upward from the moved batch. Across a few\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D14/0/code.py:27:  equally sized across the two prototypes; the generator keeps that invariant. The new\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D14/0/code.py:36:  This prevents overlap, clipping, and cross-sprite interference.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D14/0/code.py:40:  large sprite toward scale 3 and lowers cross-prototype color sharing, so the old rim\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D14/2/code.py:56:  - vary which pair is closer across generated samples, so the priority branch is not fixed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D14/2/code.py:68:  - t