ome/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/9/code.py:83:#      observable across 2-3 examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D4/1/code.py:55:- Marker color is fixed globally to blue (1). This keeps the rule easy to spot across 2-4 examples and avoids an\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D9/0/code.py:263:    Extra columns are distributed round-robin across interior gaps first, then the two outer margins,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D17/0/code.py:41:#   within one puzzle instead of relying on lucky cross-example coverage.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D17/0/code.py:47:#   dominant color across both edge columns over the whole bar height.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D17/0/code.py:71:#   output orientation difference is easy to compare across bars:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D17/0/code.py:73:# - The marker color is shared across bars and unused elsewhere, so humans can\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cbebaa4b/D14/2/code.py:46:#   Across samples, the distinctive event is obvious: colored parts close into a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D2/1/code.py:123:    Key insight: The outer edge row is the pairing key. Scale still matters for resizing, but the correct cross-group swap is determined by matching edge-color families before toggling to the companion prototype.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D8/3/code.py:18:#   intersection is the corner cell. Example for a TL corner:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D8/3/code.py:215:    - Their implied intersection is the rectangle corner.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D3/1/code.py:18:does not cut across open floor anymore; it travels on the ordered ring of boundary cells. The ring\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D3/1/code.py:67:The legend shows a left-vs-right red placement, and across examples this repeated binary cue is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/10/code.py:10:#      frame cell adjacent to the hole, tunnels through the frame, continues across the outer background,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/10/code.py:113:    \"\"\"Smallest key across all rotation/flip variants.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/10/code.py:303:    - Use only the background-colored subset of that component as the fill mask, then follow the tail's color path across the whole grid to find the linked sprite color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D9/1/code.py:34:# - The variant is visible because the four lane slots no longer form a uniform rhythm across the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D8/2/code.py:59:# - Across examples, the distinctive 5-cell plus is easy to spot and clearly no longer sits near a corner.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D8/2/code.py:168:    - rect_col = most common color across all piece pixels; all outer rectangle edges must be rect_col.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D2/2/code.py:130:    - bg = most common color. Four scale-1 prototype sprites appear: two cross-group pairs, where each pair shares one enclosed-hole count.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/62593bfd/D6/0/code.py:52:#     - Across examples, humans see the same top-vs-bottom sorting pattern as the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cbebaa4b/D10/2/code.py:48:#     - Only two connector roles/colors are used, repeated across all samples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D5/2/code.py:76:    - Outside that patch, separate monochrome sprites of the key colors are scattered across the background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D15/0/code.py:74:#       changes across rounds.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D2/3/code.py:27:#    The color logic is limited to exactly two structural roles repeated across all sprites:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cbebaa4b/D11/2/code.py:38:#    odd-red templates so the visual cue repeats across examples instead of becoming arbitrary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D3/3/code.py:42:#     - variant: it moves cell-by-cell; on touching the southeast wall it bounces to (-1,-1) and spends the remaining budget moving back across the room\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/19/code.py:21:#    across levels would force an invisible disambiguation rule and weaken the hierarchy cue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D1/0/code.py:45:#         canonical relabeling by first-seen color is stable across scales.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cbebaa4b/D6/0/code.py:45:#     the human-visible change is consistent across examples: same sprite assembly, but the output\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D1/3/code.py:43:#   explanatory pattern across samples is \"mirror certain fragments, then assemble\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D4/2/code.py:39:#       inserted into the frame interior, flush with the frame's bottom edge and centered across\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D4/2/code.py:71:#     - frames are spread across both x and y so the lower scene reads as separate boxes rather\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/62593bfd/D14/2/code.py:27:#   only two marker colors are used, repeated across all samples. That keeps the legend learnable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D1/1/code.py:59:#       - \"the rule is consistent across all sprites of the same size group\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D2/2/code.py:46:    it must carry the source lane's actual shape across the move.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D2/2/code.py:82:- Shape assignment is permuted across lanes per sample so the task cannot collapse into \"lane 0 always means plus\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D2/2/code.py:103:- The generator therefore switches to input-first construction:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D17/1/code.py:38:    - mixed directions introduce ordering risk, so the solver switches to repeated one-step simulation with fixed per-yellow directions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/4/code.py:54:#    - Samples always contain multiple frames, at least one cross-frame shared shape, and every frame's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/4/code.py:228:    - The puzzle is still driven by shape-coded color lookup, but now the lookup table is shared across\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/62593bfd/D14/1/code.py:54:#    - Across examples, the user will see a few tiny colored dots vanish while the larger shapes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D1/2/code.py:22:#       prototype identity across the row boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D1/2/code.py:49:#     - Only two prototype identities exist, repeated across the four sprites.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D1/2/code.py:141:    - The grouping cue is spatial rather than chromatic: prototype exchange happens across the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/62593bfd/D10/0/code.py:45:    - find_components(..., monochromatic=False, connectivity=8) merges touching objects even across colors, so generator must forbid 8-neighbor contact, not just overlap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D1/4/code.py:416:    # Prefer 6-12 pieces so the complement evidence repeats across multiple joins.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/5/code.py:75:#    - Across a few examples, a human can see that the number of true matching sprites\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/5/code.py:549:    \"\"\"Pick 3-5 unique near-miss shapes distributed across real bases when possible.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/28/code.py:29:#   Step 3 is the key change: the old solver expanded every sprite across 8 transforms, but that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/62593bfd/D10/1/code.py:28:# - If the generator only switched the solver to C4 but still sampled most shared\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/62593bfd/D14/3/code.py:41:#   - Marker color has repeated support across samples because every frozen object uses the same control color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/62593bfd/D14/3/code.py:47:#     This is invalid once control pixels exist, so the variant must switch to monochromatic extraction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D1/5/code.py:27:#      This variant uses two connector colors, repeated across many seams.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D1/5/code.py:49:#    - Across examples, humans can see small repeated \"connector dots\" on piece edges and also see that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D3/2/code.py:107:    Swap every sprite to the opposite prototype, then reorder the swapped sprites by scale across the existing slots.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D5/0/code.py:52:#    Humans can infer the new rule from two cues repeated across samples:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D5/0/code.py:381:    Move a triangular sprite across the grid, recoloring each visited marker into a trail.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/1/code.py:94:    - Outside the box, separated monochrome sprites of various colors are scattered across the grid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D11/1/code.py:300:    \"\"\"Split a total number of objects across shape kinds, keeping at least one per kind.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/3/code.py:33:#      across different keys, so the connector match is necessary and visually supported.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/3/code.py:51:#    - Repeated body colors across different groups make it visible that body color alone\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/3/code.py:82:    - Count the non-singleton sprites in that connector's cluster, then paint that many evenly spaced dots across the legend row using the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/1/code.py:46:#     - Humans can compare lane heights directly across 2-4 examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/1/code.py:171:    Key insight: The move is no longer announced by special marker colors; the solver must infer source and target roles by comparing visible stack heights across lanes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D16/0/code.py:62:  - Across 2-4 examples, the human-visible difference is that the marker stands alone instead of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D5/1/code.py:44:#   excessive clutter and to preserve repeated evidence across frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/22/code.py:123:    \"\"\"Smallest key across all rotation/flip variants.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D10/1/code.py:57:#       of the bars\" is stable across all examples and easy to read.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:35:#    never touch each other, and never cross the classifier boundary used by the solver.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:45:#    a separate pool and intentionally repeat across both halves so the human solver can reject color matching.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D1/2/code.py:252:    # Guarantee at least one repeated color across both halves, so color alone cannot explain the tally rows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D12/2/code.py:61:#   - `rect_col` inference assumes that color is globally most common across all piece\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D10/0/code.py:67:    - Outside that box, disconnected same-color sprites for the key colors are scattered across the grid, with an optional distractor color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/2/code.py:39:#     across lanes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/2/code.py:44:#   - Across examples, the left extreme lane consistently turns grey while the right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:28:#     every sprite switches to the opposite marker prototype at the same scale\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:49:#   - marker motion across the body, not just a color change in place\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:61:#     scales cleanly and is identical across the two prototypes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/0/code.py:73:#   is a red top marker versus a green bottom marker. Across examples, the output consistently flips\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/446ef5d2/D16/2/code.py:64:#    Across input/output examples, the user can see several fragments with the same kind\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:47:#     - Colors are deliberately reused across multiple shapes, otherwise D16 would\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:146:    \"\"\"Smallest key across all rotation/flip variants.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:543:    - Outside the frame are scattered monochrome sprites; each sprite shape matches one hole family up to rotation or reflection, and colors are reused across multiple families.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/6/code.py:551:    - Shape still determines which sprites are eligible, but color is resolved by a global shrinking inventory. Early large holes can spend a rare color and force later smaller holes to switch to a different eligible color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D4/0/code.py:66:    - Outside that box, disconnected sprites of the key colors are scattered across the background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D9/0/code.py:36:#      single example and obvious across a small ARC training set.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/0/code.py:40:#      distributes keys across separate cells, so the solver must infer row spans and column spans from full-black\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D3/0/code.py:51:#    across 2-4 examples. No color-id ordering is used; cell order is purely row-major in visi